This directory contains implementations of extra (additional) functionalities of glaunch.

Rationale
=========

The core functionality of glaunch is fairly simple and straightforward: investigating the system in search of
 GPU devices with sufficient video memory or waiting for GPUs to be released if the requirement cannot be met
 at this moment; setting up the environment variable CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES to instruct program being launched;
 and finally launching the program. Back to the time when this program was first written, this was the entire
 picture of it: all it was supposed to do is to help the users to find suitable devices for their program and
 set CUDA_VISIBLE_DEVICES accordingly.

However, more and more functionalities are introduced to glaunch since we keep finding something good to have
 in it. They were first simply stacked into a single source file, whose readability, therefore, dropped along
 with each one implemented. There was then a time point when we decided the source being unacceptably hard to
 understand, then the extra functionalities were modularized into standalone modules, one in a source file.

But modules are never truly standalone. For example, the email-notifier module will send email to the address
 configured when the program was launched, notifying about the completion of running, which would include the
 log captured by the logging module if there do exist an active one, and the timing information if there is a
 timing module currently active. This dependency requires that the email-notifier module must run after those
 modules have finished their run and had information collected. For now, this must be done by registering the
 email-notifier module after other modules manually. Furthermore, checking if certain module is active has to
 be done by iterating over all modules registered and checking their names, which retrieving information from
 other modules can only be done by acquiring a pointer to the module then accessing public members within it.

The module manager is thereby introduced to solve these questions. As a manager, it works much like the D-BUS
 daemon or the core ROS server: modules register themselves to the manager with their names and running order
 requirements, which the manager calculates executing order of modules that satisfies all requirements, keeps
 lists of active modules and protocols they implements. With the help of module manager, executing orders can
 be explicitly specified with primitive run before / after, and exchanging information with other modules can
 be achieved by sending requests to the module manager according to the protocol, which in turn get forwarded
 by the manager to all active modules implemented such protocol, with the results being forwarded back to the
 module initiates the request.


Protocol Specification
======================

A protocol is just like a programming interface, or what you call a method in D-BUS. A name together with its
 calling arguments (input parameters) and returning type (output parameters) is declared in a protocol. There
 are two types of protocol, namely standard protocols and extra protocols. Standard protocols are declared in
 glaunch-protocols.hh with an unique integral id assigned to each of them. Their arguments and returning type
 are also declared in the same file, enabling compile-time restriction via the typing system. Extra protocols
 are protocols declared individually about which the manager knows nothing in advance: when requests to these
 protocols are sent to the manager, all it does is to pack arguments passed into a std::tuple, 

Name of protocol


Module Specification
====================

A module is technically a class derived from GLaunchModule(see glaunch-module.hh) with several static methods
 implemented, or an instance of such classes. The full requirements of module type is specified as a concept,
 GLaunchModuleInterface, which is declared in glaunch-module-manager.hh.

A module can be referenced by (in cases of checking ones existence, )




Hooks Defined
=============

Hooks are declared in glaunch-hooks.hh

Checklist for adding new hooks
------------------------------

1. Add the hook id in glaunch-hooks.hh
2. (on demand) Add types used in the hook interface in glaunch-hooks.hh
3. Declare the hook interface by adding new specialization to Hook in glaunch-hooks.hh
4. Update comment at the beginning of implementation of GLaunchModuleManagerBuilder::finalize
5. Add collection of the new hook in implementation of GLaunchModuleManagerBuilder::finalize
6. Update the figure in implementation of GLaunchModuleManagerBuilder::finalize
7. Create range for it in GLaunchModuleManagerBuilder::finalize (3.5)
8. Place the create range into the array in GLaunchModuleManagerBuilder::finalize (3.6)
9. (on demand) Add partial specialization to GlaunchModuleManager::execute_hook

before_fork: std::function<void()>
----------------------------------
This hook runs right before fork(2) is called to create the process, in which the program to be launched will
 be executed later. However, note that this hook will be invoked even if fork(2) will NOT be called under the
 configurations specified.
fork(2) will not get called if and only if none of the active modules have the KeepAlive attribute set, which
 means glaunch would have nothing to do after the program get launched, rendering creation of extra processes
 meaningless and therefore can and will be omitted.

after_parent_fork: std::function<void(pid_t)>
---------------------------------------------
This hook runs right after returning from fork(2) into the parent process. The PID of child process is passed
 as its only argument. As specified for hook before_fork (see above), fork(2) may not be called under certain
 circumstances, in which case this hook will not be invoked since there will be no "parent process".

before_exec: std::function<void()>
----------------------------------
This hook runs right before exec(2) is called to actually launce the program.

after_exit: std::function<void(ExitStatus)>
-------------------------------------------
This hook runs right after the launched program (or process, to be specific) has exited. The collected status
 is passes as its only argument. It is obvious that this hook would have no change to run if the main process
 of glaunch has terminated, therefore any module that implements this hook must have KeepAlive attribute set.